{"answer":"The original expression is defined exactly for x≠2. Since x²−4=(x−2)(x+2), cancellation gives x+2 on that original domain. The simplified expression x+2 is defined at every real x, including x=2 where it equals 4; therefore it agrees with the original expression wherever the latter is defined, but the two are not equivalent as functions on all real inputs because the original has no value at x=2.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator, determine the original denominator restriction, and cancel only after retaining that restriction.","id":"factor-domain","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This directly establishes both simplification and the distinction between domain-restricted equality and global equivalence."},{"description":"Treat cancellation as creating a globally identical expression without recording the excluded input.","id":"unrestricted-cancel","outcome":"rejected","reason":"It incorrectly assigns a value to the original expression at x=2, where its denominator is zero."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by solving the original denominator equation in step-denominator.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-domain","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The domain of (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) over the reals is R minus {2}.","step_ids":["step-denominator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the difference-of-squares factorization and permitted-domain cancellation in step-factor and step-cancel.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-domain"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"restricted-simplification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x in the original domain, (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) = x + 2.","step_ids":["step-factor","step-cancel"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by comparing the original domain with the domain and value of x+2 in step-global-comparison.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-domain","restricted-simplification"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"not-global-equivalence","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The expressions are equal on the original domain but are not equivalent at every real input: x+2 has value 4 at x=2, whereas the original expression is undefined there.","step_ids":["step-global-comparison"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-denominator","justification":"A quotient is defined only when its denominator is nonzero. The equation x - 2 = 0 has the unique real solution x=2.","statement":"The original expression is undefined at x=2 and defined for every other real x."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-factor","justification":"Multiplying (x-2)(x+2) gives x^2+2x-2x-4=x^2-4.","statement":"The numerator factors as x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-denominator","step-factor"],"id":"step-cancel","justification":"For x≠2, the factor x-2 is nonzero, so ((x-2)(x+2))/(x-2)=x+2.","statement":"On the original domain, cancellation yields (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2)=x+2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-denominator","step-cancel"],"id":"step-global-comparison","justification":"The polynomial x+2 is defined for all real x and gives 2+2=4 at x=2. Step-denominator shows that the original quotient is undefined at that same input.","statement":"The cancellation preserves values for x≠2 but does not restore a value to the original expression at x=2."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
